 "result": "I couldn't get the answer. I ran 13 queries and every one failed with \"database is locked\", including a plain `SELECT 1`. Since even that fails, my queries aren't the problem. Something else is holding a lock on the shop database, and running the same query again won't change that.\n\nThe only calls that worked were the schema lookups. The query I'd run once the lock clears is:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.ordered_on, c.name\nFROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.ordered_on < '2026-10-01'\nORDER BY o.ordered_on DESC\nLIMIT 1;\n```\n\nThat assumes `ordered_on` is stored as ISO dates (`YYYY-MM-DD`). I couldn't read any rows to check, so if it uses another format, the filter needs to change.\n\nI stopped instead of retrying forever, as you'd asked, because more attempts were just spending budget with no sign the lock would clear. To unblock it, find and close whatever process has the database open for writing (another app, a stuck transaction, or a leftover `-journal`/`-wal` file), then ask me again. I'll run the query straight away.",
